IMPORT prelude

-- Evidence Act 1995 (Commonwealth of Australia) -- what the accused person meets: whether this Act governs the trial at all
-- (s 4, the Dictionary's "federal court" and "criminal proceeding"); whether I can be made to give evidence, and whether my
-- spouse, de facto partner, parent or child can be (ss 12, 13, 17, 18, 20); the privilege against self-incrimination and its
-- certificate (s 128); client legal privilege (ss 118, 119, 122, 123); hearsay as it affects me (ss 59, 60(3), 65, 66, 67);
-- my admissions (ss 81-86, 89, 90); tendency and coincidence evidence about me (ss 94, 97, 98, 100, 101); my good character
-- (ss 110, 112); the exclusions (ss 135, 137, 138, 139); and the standard of proof (s 141).
--
-- SEAT: the accused person -- the defendant in a criminal proceeding. "Can they make me give evidence? Can my partner be made
-- to? Must I answer a question that incriminates me? Can what I told the police be used against me? I was not cautioned --
-- does that matter? Can they bring up what I did before?" Provisions addressed to the court or the prosecutor are encoded as
-- what they mean for that person: what the court must or may do with evidence against me, what the prosecutor may not say.
-- Every finding, discretion or satisfaction the Act leaves to the court is asked as an input ("the court finds ..."), never
-- guessed.
--
-- Edition: the compilation registered as C2025C00340 (Compilation No. 35, compilation date 10 June 2025; Federal Register of
-- Legislation, retrieved 13 October 2026), as deposited at ../../registers/source-bundle/C2004A04858.txt.
--
-- NOT ENCODED. Chapter 2 beyond ss 12-20 (oaths, examination, documents, other evidence); Part 3.3 opinion; Part 3.4
-- beyond ss 81-86, 89, 90 (s 87 authorised admissions, s 88 proof); Part 3.5 judgments and convictions; Part 3.7 credibility;
-- Part 3.8 beyond ss 110, 112; Part 3.9 identification; Part 3.10 beyond ss 118, 119, 122, 123, 128 (s 120 unrepresented
-- parties, ss 121, 124-126 loss of privilege asked as one fact, journalist and religious privileges, s 128A, public interest
-- immunity); ss 136, 140, 142 onwards; civil proceedings throughout. A case in a State or Territory court, where the
-- State's or Territory's evidence law applies, answers `not covered`.
--
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Written from the accused's seat with the 2026-10-18 defaults. No pipeline, no coverage table, no
-- independent test pass, no human gate. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Evidence Act 1995 -- the accused person`

§§ `Questions this encoding does not cover`

-- The stable l4 has no REFUSE. A question that can reach a gap answers with an `Answer`: either `answered` with the value,
-- or `not covered` with the reason.
DECLARE `Why not covered` IS ONE OF
    -- s 4(1), Dictionary "federal court"; Judiciary Act 1903 s 79 (s 4 Note 4)
    `my case is in a State or Territory court: the evidence law of that State or Territory applies to it, picked up by s 79 of the Judiciary Act 1903 (s 4 Note 4), and that law is not encoded here`
    -- s 20(1)
    `my charge is not for an indictable offence: s 20 does not apply, and this Act says nothing else about comment on my not giving evidence`

GIVEN a IS A TYPE
GIVETH A TYPE
DECLARE `Answer` a IS ONE OF
    `answered`    HAS `the answer` IS AN a
    `not covered` HAS `because`    IS A `Why not covered`

§§ `s 4, Dictionary -- does this Act govern my trial`

-- s 4(1): "This Act applies to all proceedings in a federal court, including proceedings that: (a) relate to bail; ... (d)
-- subject to subsection (2), relate to sentencing." Dictionary: "federal court means: (a) the High Court; or (b) any other
-- court created by the Parliament (other than the Supreme Court of a Territory)". A State or Territory court -- where most
-- people charged with a Commonwealth offence are tried -- is not a federal court; s 4 Note 4 points to Judiciary Act s 79.
-- s 4(2): in a sentencing proceeding the Act applies "only if the court directs that the law of evidence applies"; (3), (4):
-- the court must direct it on a party's application about a fact significant to the sentence, or in the interests of justice
-- (asked as one fact: the court has directed). s 4(5): the Act does not apply to "(a) an appeal from a court of a State" or
-- of a Territory, or "(e) a review of a decision or order of a magistrate".
-- Dictionary: "criminal proceeding means a prosecution for an offence and includes ... committal ... bail; but does not
-- include a prosecution for an offence that is a prescribed taxation offence within the meaning of Part III of the Taxation
-- Administration Act 1953." The rules below for "a criminal proceeding" do not reach such a prosecution.
DECLARE `Does this Act govern my case` IS ONE OF
    `yes: this Act applies to my criminal proceeding (s 4(1))`
    `yes, but as a civil proceeding: a prosecution for a prescribed taxation offence is not a "criminal proceeding" under the Dictionary, so the rules for criminal proceedings in this encoding do not apply`
    `only as directed: in a sentencing proceeding the Act applies only if, and so far as, the court directs that the law of evidence applies (s 4(2))`
    `no: this Act does not apply to an appeal from a State or Territory court or to a review of a magistrate's decision (s 4(5))`

@export Does the Commonwealth Evidence Act 1995 govern the evidence in my criminal case?
GIVEN `my case is in a federal court` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your case is being heard in the High Court or another court created by the federal Parliament (for example the Federal Court of Australia or the Federal Circuit and Family Court), and not in a State or Territory court (a local or magistrates court, a District or County Court, or a State or Territory Supreme Court)
      `it is an appeal from a State or Territory court or a review of a magistrate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the hearing is an appeal from a court of a State or Territory, or a review of a magistrate's decision or order (or an appeal from such a review)
      `it is a sentencing hearing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the hearing is about the sentence to be imposed on you
      `the court has directed that the law of evidence applies` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court has directed that the law of evidence applies in the sentencing hearing (ignored if it is not a sentencing hearing)
      `I am charged with a prescribed taxation offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the charge is a prescribed taxation offence within the meaning of Part III of the Taxation Administration Act 1953
GIVETH AN `Answer` `Does this Act govern my case`
`Does this Act govern my case question` MEANS
    IF (NOT `my case is in a federal court`)
    THEN `not covered` `my case is in a State or Territory court: the evidence law of that State or Territory applies to it, picked up by s 79 of the Judiciary Act 1903 (s 4 Note 4), and that law is not encoded here`
    ELSE IF `it is an appeal from a State or Territory court or a review of a magistrate`
         THEN `answered` `no: this Act does not apply to an appeal from a State or Territory court or to a review of a magistrate's decision (s 4(5))`
         ELSE IF `it is a sentencing hearing` AND (NOT `the court has directed that the law of evidence applies`)
              THEN `answered` `only as directed: in a sentencing proceeding the Act applies only if, and so far as, the court directs that the law of evidence applies (s 4(2))`
              ELSE IF `I am charged with a prescribed taxation offence`
                   THEN `answered` `yes, but as a civil proceeding: a prosecution for a prescribed taxation offence is not a "criminal proceeding" under the Dictionary, so the rules for criminal proceedings in this encoding do not apply`
                   ELSE `answered` `yes: this Act applies to my criminal proceeding (s 4(1))`

§§ `ss 12, 13, 17 -- can I be made to give evidence (if you have been charged)`

-- s 12: "(a) every person is competent to give evidence; and (b) a person who is competent to give evidence about a fact is
-- compellable to give that evidence", "Except as otherwise provided". s 17(2): "A defendant is not competent to give evidence
-- as a witness for the prosecution." So the prosecution can never call me; for the defence I am competent but, being the
-- party who calls the defence witnesses, I choose whether to give evidence.
DECLARE `Can the prosecution make me give evidence` IS ONE OF
    `no: as a defendant I am not competent to give evidence as a witness for the prosecution, so the prosecution cannot call me (s 17(2)); whether I give evidence in my own defence is my choice`

@export Can the prosecution make me give evidence against myself at my trial?
GIVETH A `Can the prosecution make me give evidence`
`Can the prosecution make me give evidence question` MEANS
    `no: as a defendant I am not competent to give evidence as a witness for the prosecution, so the prosecution cannot call me (s 17(2)); whether I give evidence in my own defence is my choice`

-- s 13(1): not competent about a fact if, for any reason, the person lacks "the capacity to understand a question about the
-- fact" or "to give an answer that can be understood ... and that incapacity cannot be overcome". (3): not competent to give
-- sworn evidence without "the capacity to understand that, in giving evidence, he or she is under an obligation to give
-- truthful evidence"; (4), (5): then competent to give unsworn evidence "if the court has told the person" the three things
-- in s 13(5)(a)-(c). (6): competence is presumed "unless the contrary is proved" -- each input below is a finding by the court.
DECLARE `How can I give evidence` IS ONE OF
    `I am competent to give sworn evidence in my defence about this fact (ss 12(a), 13)`
    `I may give unsworn evidence about this fact once the court has told me the things in s 13(5) (ss 13(3)-(5))`
    `I am not competent to give evidence about this fact, because I cannot understand a question about it or give an understandable answer and that cannot be overcome (s 13(1)); I may still be competent about other facts (s 13(2))`

@export If I want to give evidence in my own defence, am I allowed to, and on oath?
GIVEN `the court finds I cannot understand a question about the fact` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court has found that you do not have the capacity to understand a question about the fact (for any reason, including a disability), and that this cannot be overcome
      `the court finds I cannot give an understandable answer` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court has found that you cannot give an answer that can be understood to a question about the fact, and that this cannot be overcome
      `the court finds I do not understand the duty to tell the truth` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court has found that you do not have the capacity to understand that, in giving evidence, you are under an obligation to give truthful evidence
GIVETH A `How can I give evidence`
`How can I give evidence question` MEANS
    IF `the court finds I cannot understand a question about the fact` OR `the court finds I cannot give an understandable answer`
    THEN `I am not competent to give evidence about this fact, because I cannot understand a question about it or give an understandable answer and that cannot be overcome (s 13(1)); I may still be competent about other facts (s 13(2))`
    ELSE IF `the court finds I do not understand the duty to tell the truth`
         THEN `I may give unsworn evidence about this fact once the court has told me the things in s 13(5) (ss 13(3)-(5))`
         ELSE `I am competent to give sworn evidence in my defence about this fact (ss 12(a), 13)`

-- s 17(3): "An associated defendant is not compellable to give evidence for or against a defendant in a criminal proceeding,
-- unless the associated defendant is being tried separately from the defendant." Dictionary: "associated defendant ... means a
-- person against whom a prosecution has been instituted, but not yet completed or terminated, for: (a) an offence that arose in
-- relation to the same events ...; or (b) an offence that relates to or is connected with the offence". Asked from my seat:
-- the other person is on trial and wants (or the prosecution wants) me as a witness.
DECLARE `Must I give evidence at another person's trial` IS ONE OF
    `no: I am an associated defendant being tried jointly with them, and an associated defendant is not compellable to give evidence for or against them (s 17(3))`
    `yes, if called: I am an associated defendant but I am being tried separately, so s 17(3) does not protect me and I am compellable (s 12(b)); other protections, such as s 128, still apply`
    `yes, if called: I am not an associated defendant, so I am compellable like any witness (s 12(b)); other protections, such as s 128, still apply`

@export I have been charged too. Must I give evidence at the trial of someone else charged over the same events?
GIVEN `a prosecution of me over the same or connected events is under way` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a prosecution has been started against you, and not yet completed or ended, for an offence arising from the same events as the other person's offence, or related to or connected with it
      `I am being tried separately from them` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are being tried separately from the person whose trial you are asked to give evidence at (FALSE if you are being tried together)
GIVETH A `Must I give evidence at another person's trial`
`Must I give evidence at another person's trial question` MEANS
    IF (NOT `a prosecution of me over the same or connected events is under way`)
    THEN `yes, if called: I am not an associated defendant, so I am compellable like any witness (s 12(b)); other protections, such as s 128, still apply`
    ELSE IF `I am being tried separately from them`
         THEN `yes, if called: I am an associated defendant but I am being tried separately, so s 17(3) does not protect me and I am compellable (s 12(b)); other protections, such as s 128, still apply`
         ELSE `no: I am an associated defendant being tried jointly with them, and an associated defendant is not compellable to give evidence for or against them (s 17(3))`

§§ `s 20 -- if I choose not to give evidence`

-- s 20(1): "This section applies only in a criminal proceeding for an indictable offence." (2): "The judge or any party (other
-- than the prosecutor) may comment on a failure of the defendant to give evidence. However, unless the comment is made by
-- another defendant in the proceeding, the comment must not suggest that the defendant failed to give evidence because the
-- defendant had, or believed that he or she had, committed the offence concerned."
DECLARE `What may be said about my not giving evidence` IS ONE OF
    `the prosecutor may not comment on it; the judge and any other party may, but no comment (unless made by a co-defendant) may suggest that I did not give evidence because I committed, or believed I had committed, the offence (s 20(2))`

@export If I do not give evidence, can the prosecutor or the judge comment on it to the jury?
GIVEN `I am charged with an indictable offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the offence you are being tried for is an indictable offence (one that can be tried on indictment, usually before a jury), rather than a summary offence
GIVETH AN `Answer` `What may be said about my not giving evidence`
`What may be said about my not giving evidence question` MEANS
    IF `I am charged with an indictable offence`
    THEN `answered` `the prosecutor may not comment on it; the judge and any other party may, but no comment (unless made by a co-defendant) may suggest that I did not give evidence because I committed, or believed I had committed, the offence (s 20(2))`
    ELSE `not covered` `my charge is not for an indictable offence: s 20 does not apply, and this Act says nothing else about comment on my not giving evidence`

§§ `ss 18, 20(3)-(4) -- if the prosecution wants my spouse, partner, parent or child as a witness`

-- s 18(2): "A person who, when required to give evidence, is the spouse, de facto partner, parent or child of a defendant may
-- object to being required: (a) to give evidence; or (b) to give evidence of a communication between the person and the
-- defendant; as a witness for the prosecution." (3): the objection is made "before the person gives the evidence or as soon
-- as practicable after the person becomes aware of the right". (6): the person "must not be required to give the evidence if
-- the court finds that: (a) there is a likelihood that harm would or might be caused ... to the person, or to the relationship
-- between the person and the defendant ...; and (b) the nature and extent of that harm outweighs the desirability of having
-- the evidence given". (8): "the prosecutor may not comment on" the objection, the decision, or the failure to give evidence.
-- "child" and "parent" include adopted, ex-nuptial and household children and parents (Dictionary Part 2 cl 10); "de facto
-- partner" is a couple not legally married (cl 11) -- asked as a fact. Former section 19 (offences where no objection could be
-- made) was repealed by Act No. 113, 2015 (endnotes): the objection is open whatever the charge.
DECLARE `Must my family member give evidence for the prosecution` IS ONE OF
    `no special rule: they are not my spouse, de facto partner, parent or child when required, so s 18 gives them no objection and they are compellable (s 12(b))`
    `yes, unless they object: they may object, before giving the evidence or as soon as practicable after learning of the right (s 18(2), (3))`
    `no: they objected and the court found that harm to them or to our relationship is likely and outweighs the desirability of their evidence, so they must not be required to give it (s 18(6)); the prosecutor may not comment on it (s 18(8))`
    `yes: they objected but the court did not find that the likely harm outweighs the desirability of their evidence, so they may be required to give it (s 18(6)); the prosecutor may not comment on the objection or the decision (s 18(8))`

@export Can my spouse, partner, parent or child be made to give evidence against me for the prosecution?
GIVEN `they are my spouse, de facto partner, parent or child` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, at the time they are required to give evidence, the person is your husband or wife, your de facto partner, your parent or your child (adopted, ex-nuptial and household children and parents count)
      `they have objected` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether they have objected to giving the evidence (or to giving evidence of a communication between you and them)
      `the court finds harm to them or our relationship is likely` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court has found a likelihood that harm would or might be caused, directly or indirectly, to them or to your relationship with them, if they give the evidence
      `the court finds the harm outweighs having the evidence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court has found that the nature and extent of that harm outweighs the desirability of having the evidence given
GIVETH A `Must my family member give evidence for the prosecution`
`Must my family member give evidence for the prosecution question` MEANS
    IF (NOT `they are my spouse, de facto partner, parent or child`)
    THEN `no special rule: they are not my spouse, de facto partner, parent or child when required, so s 18 gives them no objection and they are compellable (s 12(b))`
    ELSE IF (NOT `they have objected`)
         THEN `yes, unless they object: they may object, before giving the evidence or as soon as practicable after learning of the right (s 18(2), (3))`
         ELSE IF `the court finds harm to them or our relationship is likely` AND `the court finds the harm outweighs having the evidence`
              THEN `no: they objected and the court found that harm to them or to our relationship is likely and outweighs the desirability of their evidence, so they must not be required to give it (s 18(6)); the prosecutor may not comment on it (s 18(8))`
              ELSE `yes: they objected but the court did not find that the likely harm outweighs the desirability of their evidence, so they may be required to give it (s 18(6)); the prosecutor may not comment on the objection or the decision (s 18(8))`

§§ `s 128 -- if I am asked a question that may incriminate me`

-- s 128(1): applies "if a witness objects to giving particular evidence ... on the ground that the evidence may tend to prove
-- that the witness: (a) has committed an offence against or arising under an Australian law or a law of a foreign country; or
-- (b) is liable to a civil penalty." (2): "The court must determine whether or not there are reasonable grounds for the
-- objection." (3): if there are, "the court is not to require the witness to give the evidence" and tells the witness of the
-- certificate. (4): it "may require the witness to give the evidence if the court is satisfied that: (a) the evidence does not
-- tend to prove ... an offence against or ... a civil penalty under, a law of a foreign country; and (b) the interests of
-- justice require". (5): evidence given willingly or under (4) earns a certificate. (6): a certificate also follows if the
-- objection was overruled and the court later finds there were reasonable grounds.
-- (10): "In a criminal proceeding, this section does not apply in relation to the giving of evidence by a defendant, being
-- evidence that the defendant: (a) did an act the doing of which is a fact in issue; or (b) had a state of mind the existence of
-- which is a fact in issue." So in the witness box at my own trial I cannot refuse to answer about the very acts and state of
-- mind I am charged over.
DECLARE `Must I answer this question` IS ONE OF
    `yes: I am the defendant and the question is about an act I did, or a state of mind I had, that is a fact in issue in my own trial, so s 128 does not apply to it (s 128(10))`
    `yes: I have not objected; s 128 applies only if I object on the ground that the answer may tend to prove an offence or a civil penalty (s 128(1))`
    `yes: the court determined there are no reasonable grounds for my objection (s 128(2)); if it later finds there were, I must be given a certificate (s 128(6))`
    `no: there are reasonable grounds for my objection, so I need not answer unless the court requires me to; if I answer willingly I must be given a certificate (s 128(3), (5))`
    `yes, with a certificate: the court is satisfied that the answer does not tend to prove a foreign offence or penalty and that the interests of justice require it, so it may require me to answer, and I must be given a certificate (s 128(4), (5))`

@export I am a witness and have been asked a question whose answer may show I committed an offence. Must I answer?
GIVEN `I am the defendant and it is about an act or state of mind in issue at my trial` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are the defendant in this criminal case and the question is about whether you did an act, or failed to act, or had a state of mind, that is itself a fact in issue in your trial
      `I have objected that the answer may incriminate me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have objected to answering on the ground that the answer may tend to prove you committed an offence under Australian or foreign law, or are liable to a civil penalty
      `the court finds reasonable grounds for my objection` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court has determined that there are reasonable grounds for your objection
      `the answer may tend to prove a foreign offence or penalty` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the answer may tend to prove that you committed an offence, or are liable to a civil penalty, under the law of a foreign country
      `the court is satisfied justice requires my answer` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court is satisfied that the interests of justice require you to give the evidence
GIVETH A `Must I answer this question`
`Must I answer this question question` MEANS
    IF `I am the defendant and it is about an act or state of mind in issue at my trial`
    THEN `yes: I am the defendant and the question is about an act I did, or a state of mind I had, that is a fact in issue in my own trial, so s 128 does not apply to it (s 128(10))`
    ELSE IF (NOT `I have objected that the answer may incriminate me`)
         THEN `yes: I have not objected; s 128 applies only if I object on the ground that the answer may tend to prove an offence or a civil penalty (s 128(1))`
         ELSE IF (NOT `the court finds reasonable grounds for my objection`)
              THEN `yes: the court determined there are no reasonable grounds for my objection (s 128(2)); if it later finds there were, I must be given a certificate (s 128(6))`
              ELSE IF (NOT `the answer may tend to prove a foreign offence or penalty`) AND `the court is satisfied justice requires my answer`
                   THEN `yes, with a certificate: the court is satisfied that the answer does not tend to prove a foreign offence or penalty and that the interests of justice require it, so it may require me to answer, and I must be given a certificate (s 128(4), (5))`
                   ELSE `no: there are reasonable grounds for my objection, so I need not answer unless the court requires me to; if I answer willingly I must be given a certificate (s 128(3), (5))`

-- s 128(7): "In any proceeding in an Australian court: (a) evidence given by a person in respect of which a certificate under
-- this section has been given; and (b) evidence of any information, document or thing obtained as a direct or indirect
-- consequence of the person having given evidence; cannot be used against the person. However, this does not apply to a
-- criminal proceeding in respect of the falsity of the evidence." (8): despite any challenge to the certificate. (9): "If a
-- defendant in a criminal proceeding for an offence is given a certificate ..., subsection (7) does not apply in a proceeding
-- that is a retrial of the defendant for the same offence or a trial of the defendant for an offence arising out of the same
-- facts". (12)-(14): a NSW s 128 certificate has the same effect in a federal court and in a Commonwealth prosecution.
@export I gave evidence under a s 128 certificate. Can that evidence, or what was found because of it, be used against me now?
GIVEN `I was given a certificate for that evidence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were given a certificate under s 128 of this Act (or under s 128 of the Evidence Act 1995 (NSW)) for the evidence
      `this proceeding is about whether that evidence was false` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the proceeding in which it is to be used is a criminal proceeding about the falsity of that evidence (for example perjury)
      `I got the certificate as a defendant and this is a retrial or a trial on the same facts` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were given the certificate as the defendant in a criminal trial, and the present proceeding is a retrial of you for the same offence or a trial for an offence arising out of the same facts
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can my certified evidence be used against me` IF
        (NOT `I was given a certificate for that evidence`)
    OR  `this proceeding is about whether that evidence was false`
    OR  `I got the certificate as a defendant and this is a retrial or a trial on the same facts`

§§ `ss 118, 119, 122, 123 -- what I told my lawyer`

-- s 118: "Evidence is not to be adduced if, on objection by a client, the court finds that adducing the evidence would result
-- in disclosure of: (a) a confidential communication made between the client and a lawyer; ... (c) the contents of a
-- confidential document ... for the dominant purpose of the lawyer ... providing legal advice to the client." s 119: the same
-- for communications and documents "for the dominant purpose of the client being provided with professional legal services
-- relating to an Australian or overseas proceeding ... in which the client is or may be ... a party". s 122(1), (2): lost if I
-- consent, or act inconsistently with objecting -- (3)(a) "knowingly and voluntarily disclosed the substance of the evidence to
-- another person". Sections 121, 124-126 (other losses) are asked as one fact. s 123: this Division "does not prevent a
-- defendant from adducing evidence" unless it is an associated defendant's communication with that person's own lawyer.
DECLARE `Is what I told my lawyer protected` IS ONE OF
    `no: the communication or document was not confidential, or not made for the dominant purpose of legal advice or of legal services for a proceeding, so ss 118 and 119 do not protect it`
    `not unless I object: client legal privilege works on objection by the client (ss 118, 119)`
    `no: the privilege is lost because I consented, or acted inconsistently with objecting, for example by knowingly and voluntarily disclosing its substance to someone else (s 122(1)-(3))`
    `no: the privilege is lost under one of ss 121, 124 to 126`
    `yes: on my objection, evidence of it is not to be adduced (ss 118, 119)`

@export Can the prosecution use what I told my lawyer, or documents prepared for my legal advice or defence?
GIVEN `it was confidential` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the communication or document was confidential (made or prepared in circumstances in which it was to be kept confidential)
      `its dominant purpose was legal advice or my case` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it was made or prepared for the dominant purpose of a lawyer giving you legal advice, or of you being given legal services about a court case you are, or might be, a party to
      `I object to it being given in evidence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you (or your lawyer for you) object to the evidence being given
      `I consented, or disclosed its substance to someone else` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you consented to the evidence being given, or knowingly and voluntarily told someone else its substance, or otherwise acted in a way inconsistent with objecting (a disclosure made under duress, by deception, under compulsion of law, or to someone sharing your interest in the case does not count)
      `another loss of privilege applies` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether one of ss 121, 124, 125 or 126 applies (for example a communication made in furtherance of an offence or fraud, s 125)
GIVETH A `Is what I told my lawyer protected`
`Is what I told my lawyer protected question` MEANS
    IF (NOT `it was confidential`) OR (NOT `its dominant purpose was legal advice or my case`)
    THEN `no: the communication or document was not confidential, or not made for the dominant purpose of legal advice or of legal services for a proceeding, so ss 118 and 119 do not protect it`
    ELSE IF `I consented, or disclosed its substance to someone else`
         THEN `no: the privilege is lost because I consented, or acted inconsistently with objecting, for example by knowingly and voluntarily disclosing its substance to someone else (s 122(1)-(3))`
         ELSE IF `another loss of privilege applies`
              THEN `no: the privilege is lost under one of ss 121, 124 to 126`
              ELSE IF (NOT `I object to it being given in evidence`)
                   THEN `not unless I object: client legal privilege works on objection by the client (ss 118, 119)`
                   ELSE `yes: on my objection, evidence of it is not to be adduced (ss 118, 119)`

@export Can client legal privilege stop me using evidence I need in my defence?
GIVEN `it is a co-accused's communication with their own lawyer about their prosecution` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the evidence is of a confidential communication between an associated defendant (someone being prosecuted over the same or connected events) and their own lawyer about their prosecution, or of a confidential document they or their lawyer prepared for it
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can privilege stop me adducing this evidence` IF
    `it is a co-accused's communication with their own lawyer about their prosecution`

§§ `ss 59, 60, 65, 66, 67 -- if the prosecution relies on what someone said out of court`

-- s 59(1), the hearsay rule: "Evidence of a previous representation made by a person is not admissible to prove the existence
-- of a fact that it can reasonably be supposed that the person intended to assert by the representation." s 60(1): it "does
-- not apply to evidence of a previous representation that is admitted because it is relevant for a purpose other than proof of
-- an asserted fact" -- whether it is so admitted is the court's finding.
-- s 65 (maker not available, Dictionary Part 2 cl 4: dead, not competent, unable, unlawful, prohibited, or not found or
-- compelled despite all reasonable steps): (2) first-hand evidence -- "given by a person who saw, heard or otherwise perceived
-- the representation being made" -- if the representation was "(a) made under a duty ...; (b) made when or shortly after the
-- asserted fact occurred and in circumstances that make it unlikely that the representation is a fabrication; (c) ... highly
-- probable that the representation is reliable; or (d) ... against the interests of the person who made it ... and ... likely
-- ... reliable" (the circumstances asked as one court finding); (3) evidence given in an earlier proceeding where I
-- "cross-examined the person ... or had a reasonable opportunity to". s 67(1): ss 65(2), (3), (8) "do not apply to evidence
-- adduced by a party unless that party has given reasonable notice in writing to each other party", unless (4) the court
-- directs otherwise.
-- s 66 (maker available): (2) if the maker "has been or is to be called", first-hand evidence of it is not barred "if, when the
-- representation was made, the occurrence of the asserted fact was fresh in the memory" of the maker; (3) but not for the
-- prosecutor if the representation "was made for the purpose of indicating the evidence that the person ... would be able to
-- give" (a witness statement) "unless the representation concerns the identity of a person, place or thing".
-- Other exceptions (business records s 69, and the rest) and the discretions are asked as one fact.
DECLARE `Can the prosecution prove this by an out-of-court statement` IS ONE OF
    `the hearsay rule does not stop it: the statement is admitted for a purpose other than proving the fact asserted (s 60(1))`
    `the hearsay rule does not stop it: another exception to the hearsay rule applies`
    `the hearsay rule does not stop it: the maker is not available and the first-hand evidence meets s 65(2), with notice given (ss 65(2), 67)`
    `the hearsay rule does not stop it: the maker gave the evidence in an earlier proceeding where I cross-examined, or could have, with notice given (ss 65(3), 67)`
    `the hearsay rule does not stop it: the maker is called, the evidence is first-hand, and the fact was fresh in their memory when they made it (s 66(2))`
    `no: the hearsay rule excludes it to prove the fact asserted (s 59(1))`

@export Can the prosecution use what someone said out of court (for example to the police) to prove what they said happened?
GIVEN `the court admits it for another purpose` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court admits the statement because it is relevant for a purpose other than proving the fact the maker intended to assert (for example to show that the words were said)
      `another hearsay exception applies` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether an exception to the hearsay rule other than ss 60, 65 and 66 applies (for example business records, s 69)
      `the maker is available to give evidence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person who made the statement is available to give evidence (FALSE if they are dead, not competent, unable, or cannot be found or compelled despite all reasonable steps)
      `the witness saw or heard the statement being made` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the evidence of the statement is given by someone who saw, heard or otherwise perceived it being made, or (for an earlier court hearing) is the transcript or recording of it
      `the court finds a s 65(2) circumstance` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court finds the statement was made under a duty, or when or shortly after the event and in circumstances making fabrication unlikely, or in circumstances making reliability highly probable, or against the maker's interests and in circumstances making reliability likely
      `it was evidence in an earlier hearing where I could cross-examine` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the statement was made in giving evidence in an earlier court proceeding in which you cross-examined the maker about it, or had a reasonable opportunity to
      `the prosecution gave me written notice or the court excused it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the prosecution gave you reasonable notice in writing that it would rely on s 65, or the court directed that s 65 applies without notice
      `the maker is being called as a witness` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person who made the statement has been or is to be called to give evidence
      `the event was fresh in their memory when they made it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court finds that the event was fresh in the maker's memory when they made the statement
      `it was a statement of the evidence they could give, not about identity` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the statement was made to indicate the evidence the maker could give in a court case (such as a witness statement to police), and it is not about the identity of a person, place or thing
GIVETH A `Can the prosecution prove this by an out-of-court statement`
`Can the prosecution prove this by an out-of-court statement question` MEANS
    IF `the court admits it for another purpose`
    THEN `the hearsay rule does not stop it: the statement is admitted for a purpose other than proving the fact asserted (s 60(1))`
    ELSE IF `another hearsay exception applies`
         THEN `the hearsay rule does not stop it: another exception to the hearsay rule applies`
         ELSE IF (NOT `the maker is available to give evidence`) AND `the witness saw or heard the statement being made` AND `the court finds a s 65(2) circumstance` AND `the prosecution gave me written notice or the court excused it`
              THEN `the hearsay rule does not stop it: the maker is not available and the first-hand evidence meets s 65(2), with notice given (ss 65(2), 67)`
              ELSE IF (NOT `the maker is available to give evidence`) AND `it was evidence in an earlier hearing where I could cross-examine` AND `the prosecution gave me written notice or the court excused it`
                   THEN `the hearsay rule does not stop it: the maker gave the evidence in an earlier proceeding where I cross-examined, or could have, with notice given (ss 65(3), 67)`
                   ELSE IF `the maker is available to give evidence` AND `the maker is being called as a witness` AND `the witness saw or heard the statement being made` AND `the event was fresh in their memory when they made it` AND (NOT `it was a statement of the evidence they could give, not about identity`)
                        THEN `the hearsay rule does not stop it: the maker is called, the evidence is first-hand, and the fact was fresh in their memory when they made it (s 66(2))`
                        ELSE `no: the hearsay rule excludes it to prove the fact asserted (s 59(1))`

-- s 65(8): "The hearsay rule does not apply to: (a) evidence of a previous representation adduced by a defendant if the evidence
-- is given by a person who saw, heard or otherwise perceived the representation being made; or (b) a document tendered as
-- evidence by a defendant so far as it contains a previous representation". No s 65(2) circumstance is needed; the s 67 notice
-- is (s 67(1)). (9): if I do, the prosecution may answer with first-hand evidence of another representation about the matter.
@export The person who told someone something helpful to me is not available. Can I use what they said in my defence?
GIVEN `the maker is available to give evidence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person who made the statement is available to give evidence (FALSE if they are dead, not competent, unable, or cannot be found or compelled despite all reasonable steps)
      `my witness saw or heard it, or it is in a document I tender` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the evidence of the statement is given by someone who saw, heard or otherwise perceived it being made, or is in a document you tender
      `I gave written notice or the court excused it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you gave the prosecution reasonable notice in writing that you would rely on s 65(8), or the court directed that it applies without notice
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can I use an absent person's statement in my defence` IF
        (NOT `the maker is available to give evidence`)
    AND `my witness saw or heard it, or it is in a document I tender`
    AND `I gave written notice or the court excused it`

§§ `ss 81-86, 89, 90 -- if the prosecution relies on something I said (an admission)`

-- Dictionary: "admission means a previous representation that is: (a) made by a person who is or becomes a party to a
-- proceeding (including a defendant in a criminal proceeding); and (b) adverse to the person's interest in the outcome".
-- s 81(1): "The hearsay rule and the opinion rule do not apply to evidence of an admission." s 82: but they do "unless: (a) it
-- is given by a person who saw, heard or otherwise perceived the admission being made; or (b) it is a document in which the
-- admission is made". s 60(3): the non-hearsay purpose route "does not apply in a criminal proceeding to evidence of an
-- admission".
-- s 84(1): "not admissible unless the court is satisfied that the admission, and the making of the admission, were not
-- influenced by: (a) violent, oppressive, inhuman or degrading conduct ...; or (b) a threat of conduct of that kind"; (2) only
-- if I have "raised in the proceeding an issue" about it.
-- s 85(1): applies to my admission made "(a) to, or in the presence of, an investigating official who at that time was
-- performing functions in connection with the investigation ...; or (b) as a result of an act of another person who was, and
-- who the defendant knew or reasonably believed to be, capable of influencing the decision whether a prosecution ... should be
-- brought or ... continued". (2): "not admissible unless the circumstances ... were such as to make it unlikely that the truth
-- of the admission was adversely affected". Dictionary: "investigating official" is a police officer, or a person appointed
-- under an Australian law with functions of preventing or investigating offences, in either case not one engaged in covert
-- investigations under a superior's orders.
-- s 90: the court "may refuse to admit evidence of an admission ... if: (a) the evidence is adduced by the prosecution; and (b)
-- having regard to the circumstances in which the admission was made, it would be unfair to a defendant to use the evidence."
-- s 138 and s 139 (improperly obtained, no caution) follow in the next group; they are asked separately.
DECLARE `Can my admission be used against me` IS ONE OF
    `no: the evidence of it is not first-hand (not from someone who saw or heard me make it, nor a document in which I made it), so the hearsay rule applies to it (s 82)`
    `no: I raised the issue and the court is not satisfied that it was uninfluenced by violent, oppressive, inhuman or degrading conduct or a threat of it (s 84)`
    `no: it was made to or in the presence of an investigator (or because of someone I knew could influence whether I was prosecuted), and the court does not find the circumstances made it unlikely its truth was adversely affected (s 85(2))`
    `no: the court refuses it because using it would be unfair to me in the circumstances in which I made it (s 90)`
    `yes, so far as ss 81 to 85 and 90 go: the hearsay and opinion rules do not apply to it (s 81(1)); s 138 and the other exclusions can still apply`

@export Can something I said (an admission) be used as evidence against me?
GIVEN `the witness heard me say it, or it is in my own document` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the evidence of what you said comes from someone who saw, heard or otherwise perceived you saying it (or is a document in which you made it, such as a signed statement or your own message), rather than from someone told about it second-hand
      `I have raised violence, oppression or threats` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you (through your lawyer) have raised in the case an issue whether the admission was influenced by violent, oppressive, inhuman or degrading conduct, or a threat of it, towards you or anyone else
      `the court is satisfied it was not influenced by violence or threats` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court is satisfied that the admission, and your making it, were not influenced by that conduct or threat (ignored if you have not raised it)
      `I said it to or in front of an investigator, or because of someone who could decide my prosecution` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you said it to, or in the presence of, a police officer or other investigating official who was then investigating an offence; or because of something done by a person you knew or reasonably believed could influence whether you would be prosecuted
      `the court finds the circumstances make it unlikely its truth was affected` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court finds that the circumstances in which you said it (your age, personality, education, any disability, the questions, and any threat, promise or inducement) make it unlikely that its truth was adversely affected
      `the court finds it would be unfair to me to use it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court finds that, having regard to the circumstances in which you said it, it would be unfair to you to use it, and refuses it
GIVETH A `Can my admission be used against me`
`Can my admission be used against me question` MEANS
    IF (NOT `the witness heard me say it, or it is in my own document`)
    THEN `no: the evidence of it is not first-hand (not from someone who saw or heard me make it, nor a document in which I made it), so the hearsay rule applies to it (s 82)`
    ELSE IF `I have raised violence, oppression or threats` AND (NOT `the court is satisfied it was not influenced by violence or threats`)
         THEN `no: I raised the issue and the court is not satisfied that it was uninfluenced by violent, oppressive, inhuman or degrading conduct or a threat of it (s 84)`
         ELSE IF `I said it to or in front of an investigator, or because of someone who could decide my prosecution` AND (NOT `the court finds the circumstances make it unlikely its truth was affected`)
              THEN `no: it was made to or in the presence of an investigator (or because of someone I knew could influence whether I was prosecuted), and the court does not find the circumstances made it unlikely its truth was adversely affected (s 85(2))`
              ELSE IF `the court finds it would be unfair to me to use it`
                   THEN `no: the court refuses it because using it would be unfair to me in the circumstances in which I made it (s 90)`
                   ELSE `yes, so far as ss 81 to 85 and 90 go: the hearsay and opinion rules do not apply to it (s 81(1)); s 138 and the other exclusions can still apply`

-- s 86(1): "only if an oral admission was made by a defendant to an investigating official in response to a question put or a
-- representation made by the official." (2): "A document prepared by or on behalf of the official is not admissible to prove
-- the contents of the question, representation or response unless the defendant has acknowledged that the document is a true
-- record"; (3) "by signing, initialling or otherwise marking the document". (4): "document does not include" a sound or video
-- recording or its transcript. So a police officer's notebook of my oral answers needs my mark; a recording does not.
@export The police wrote down what I said in answer to their questions. Can their written record be used to prove what I said?
GIVEN `it is a sound or video recording, or a transcript of one` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the record is a sound recording or a video recording (with sound) of the questioning, or a transcript of such a recording, rather than the officer's notes or typed record
      `I signed, initialled or marked it as a true record` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you acknowledged the written record as a true record of the questions and your answers by signing, initialling or otherwise marking it
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can the police record of my oral answers prove what I said` IF
        `it is a sound or video recording, or a transcript of one`
    OR  `I signed, initialled or marked it as a true record`

-- s 89(1): "an inference unfavourable to a party must not be drawn from evidence that the party ... failed or refused: (a) to
-- answer one or more questions; or (b) to respond to a representation; put or made ... by an investigating official who at that
-- time was performing functions in connection with the investigation". (2): "Evidence of that kind is not admissible if it can
-- only be used to draw such an inference." (3): it may be used "to prove that the party ... failed or refused ... if the failure
-- or refusal is a fact in issue". (4): "inference includes: (a) an inference of consciousness of guilt; or (b) an inference
-- relevant to a party's credibility."
DECLARE `Can my silence be used against me` IS ONE OF
    `no: no unfavourable inference (of guilt, or about my credibility) may be drawn from my not answering the investigator, and evidence of it is not admissible if that is its only use (s 89(1), (2), (4))`
    `only to prove the refusal itself: my refusal is a fact in issue, so it may be proved, but still no unfavourable inference may be drawn from it (s 89(1), (3))`
    `s 89 does not protect it: I was not questioned by an investigating official who was then investigating an offence`

@export I refused to answer police questions. Can my silence be used against me?
GIVEN `the questions were put by an investigator then investigating an offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the questions were put to you by a police officer or other investigating official who was then performing functions in investigating an offence (not someone working undercover under orders, and not a private person)
      `my refusal is itself a fact in issue` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the failure or refusal to answer is itself something the case is about (for example where the charge is failing to answer a lawful requirement)
GIVETH A `Can my silence be used against me`
`Can my silence be used against me question` MEANS
    IF (NOT `the questions were put by an investigator then investigating an offence`)
    THEN `s 89 does not protect it: I was not questioned by an investigating official who was then investigating an offence`
    ELSE IF `my refusal is itself a fact in issue`
         THEN `only to prove the refusal itself: my refusal is a fact in issue, so it may be proved, but still no unfavourable inference may be drawn from it (s 89(1), (3))`
         ELSE `no: no unfavourable inference (of guilt, or about my credibility) may be drawn from my not answering the investigator, and evidence of it is not admissible if that is its only use (s 89(1), (2), (4))`

-- s 83(1): s 81 "does not prevent the application of the hearsay rule or the opinion rule to evidence of an admission in respect
-- of the case of a third party"; (2) "may be used in respect of the case of a third party if that party consents"; (3) consent
-- "cannot be given in respect of part only". Asked from my seat: the admission is a co-accused's.
@export My co-accused admitted something to the police. Can their admission be used in the case against me?
GIVEN `I consent to it being used in my case` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you consent to the whole of the co-accused's admission being used in respect of your case (consent cannot be given to part only)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can a co-accused's admission be used in my case` IF
    `I consent to it being used in my case`

§§ `ss 138, 139 -- if the evidence was obtained improperly or illegally, or I was not cautioned`

-- s 139(1): evidence of a statement or act "during questioning is taken to have been obtained improperly if: (a) the person was
-- under arrest for an offence at the time; and (b) the questioning was conducted by an investigating official who was at the
-- time empowered ... to arrest the person; and (c) before starting the questioning the investigating official did not caution
-- the person that the person does not have to say or do anything but that anything the person does say or do may be used in
-- evidence." (2): where the official had no power to arrest me, after the official "formed a belief that there was sufficient
-- evidence to establish that the person has committed an offence", without the caution. (3): "The caution must be given in, or
-- translated into, a language in which the person is able to communicate with reasonable fluency". (4): none of this applies
-- "so far as any Australian law requires the person to answer questions". (5): "under arrest" includes being "in the company
-- of an investigating official for the purpose of being questioned, if: (a) the official believes that there is sufficient
-- evidence to establish that the person has committed an offence ...; or (b) the official would not allow the person to leave
-- ...; or (c) the official has given the person reasonable grounds for believing that the person would not be allowed to
-- leave". (6): not so "only because of subsection (5)" if (a) the official is performing border functions and does not
-- believe I committed a Commonwealth offence, or (b) "is exercising a power under an Australian law to detain and search the
-- person or to require the person to provide information or to answer questions".
GIVEN `I was under arrest` IS A BOOLEAN
      `I was held for questioning in a way s 139(5) treats as arrest` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the official was exercising a border, search or information power` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the official could arrest me` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the official believed there was enough evidence I had committed an offence` IS A BOOLEAN
      `I was cautioned in a language I speak reasonably fluently` IS A BOOLEAN
      `a law required me to answer` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 139 takes it to be improperly obtained` IF
        (NOT `a law required me to answer`)
    AND (NOT `I was cautioned in a language I speak reasonably fluently`)
    AND (    (    `the official could arrest me`
              AND (    `I was under arrest`
                   OR (`I was held for questioning in a way s 139(5) treats as arrest` AND (NOT `the official was exercising a border, search or information power`))))
         OR  ((NOT `the official could arrest me`) AND `the official believed there was enough evidence I had committed an offence`))

@export I was questioned by police without being cautioned. Is what I said or did taken to have been obtained improperly?
GIVEN `I was under arrest` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were under arrest for an offence when you were questioned
      `I was held for questioning in a way s 139(5) treats as arrest` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, though not formally arrested, you were with the official to be questioned and the official believed there was enough evidence that you had committed the offence, or would not have let you leave, or gave you reasonable grounds to believe you would not be allowed to leave
      `the official was exercising a border, search or information power` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the official was dealing with people or goods entering or leaving Australia and did not believe you had committed a Commonwealth offence, or was exercising a legal power to detain and search you or to require you to give information or answer questions
      `the official could arrest me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the official questioning you had, because of their office, the power to arrest you (a police officer does)
      `the official believed there was enough evidence I had committed an offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, before you made the statement or did the act, the official had formed a belief that there was sufficient evidence to establish that you had committed an offence
      `I was cautioned in a language I speak reasonably fluently` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, before the questioning (or before the statement or act), you were told that you do not have to say or do anything but that anything you say or do may be used in evidence, in or translated into a language in which you can communicate with reasonable fluency (in writing if you cannot hear adequately)
      `a law required me to answer` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a law required you to answer the questions or do the things asked
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is my uncautioned statement taken to be improperly obtained` IF
    `s 139 takes it to be improperly obtained` `I was under arrest` `I was held for questioning in a way s 139(5) treats as arrest` `the official was exercising a border, search or information power` `the official could arrest me` `the official believed there was enough evidence I had committed an offence` `I was cautioned in a language I speak reasonably fluently` `a law required me to answer`

-- s 138(1): "Evidence that was obtained: (a) improperly or in contravention of an Australian law; or (b) in consequence of an
-- impropriety or of a contravention of an Australian law; is not to be admitted unless the desirability of admitting the
-- evidence outweighs the undesirability of admitting evidence that has been obtained in the way in which the evidence was
-- obtained." (2): an admission (and evidence obtained in consequence of it) "is taken to have been obtained improperly if the
-- person conducting the questioning: (a) did, or omitted to do, an act ... even though he or she knew or ought reasonably to
-- have known that the act or omission was likely to impair substantially the ability of the person being questioned to respond
-- rationally ...; or (b) made a false statement ... even though he or she knew or ought reasonably to have known that the
-- statement was false and that making the false statement was likely to cause the person ... to make an admission." (3): the
-- matters the court weighs (probative value, importance, gravity, deliberateness, ICCPR rights, difficulty of obtaining it
-- properly) -- the balance itself is the court's, asked as one finding. The burden is on the prosecution: it is "not to be
-- admitted unless".
DECLARE `Must improperly obtained evidence be excluded` IS ONE OF
    `s 138 does not exclude it: it was not obtained improperly or illegally, or in consequence of an impropriety or illegality`
    `yes: it was obtained improperly or illegally, and it is not to be admitted, because the court does not find that the desirability of admitting it outweighs the undesirability of admitting evidence obtained that way (s 138(1))`
    `no: it was obtained improperly or illegally, but the court finds the desirability of admitting it outweighs the undesirability of admitting evidence obtained that way, so it may be admitted (s 138(1), (3))`

@export The evidence against me was obtained improperly or illegally. Must the court exclude it?
GIVEN `it was obtained improperly or illegally` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the evidence was obtained improperly or in breach of an Australian law (for example in breach of the Crimes Act 1914's rules on detention and questioning, or without a valid warrant), or in consequence of such an impropriety or breach
      `the questioner knowingly impaired my ability to respond rationally` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it is an admission made in questioning, or found because of one, and the questioner did or failed to do something they knew or ought reasonably to have known was likely to impair substantially your ability to respond rationally
      `the questioner knowingly made a false statement likely to make me admit` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it is an admission made in questioning, or found because of one, and the questioner made a statement they knew or ought reasonably to have known was false and likely to cause you to make an admission
      `I was under arrest` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it is something you said or did during questioning while you were under arrest for an offence
      `I was held for questioning in a way s 139(5) treats as arrest` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, though not formally arrested, you were with the official to be questioned and the official believed there was enough evidence that you had committed the offence, or would not have let you leave, or gave you reasonable grounds to believe you would not be allowed to leave
      `the official was exercising a border, search or information power` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the official was dealing with people or goods entering or leaving Australia and did not believe you had committed a Commonwealth offence, or was exercising a legal power to detain and search you or to require you to give information or answer questions
      `the official could arrest me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the official questioning you had, because of their office, the power to arrest you
      `the official believed there was enough evidence I had committed an offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, before you made the statement or did the act, the official had formed a belief that there was sufficient evidence to establish that you had committed an offence
      `I was cautioned in a language I speak reasonably fluently` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were given the caution (that you need not say or do anything, but anything you say or do may be used in evidence) in time, in a language you can communicate in with reasonable fluency (TRUE if the evidence did not come from questioning)
      `a law required me to answer` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a law required you to answer the questions or do the things asked
      `the court finds admitting it is more desirable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court finds that the desirability of admitting the evidence outweighs the undesirability of admitting evidence obtained in that way (weighing its probative value and importance, the nature of the offence, the gravity of the impropriety, whether it was deliberate or reckless, and the other s 138(3) matters)
GIVETH A `Must improperly obtained evidence be excluded`
`Must improperly obtained evidence be excluded question` MEANS
    IF NOT (    `it was obtained improperly or illegally`
             OR `the questioner knowingly impaired my ability to respond rationally`
             OR `the questioner knowingly made a false statement likely to make me admit`
             OR `s 139 takes it to be improperly obtained` `I was under arrest` `I was held for questioning in a way s 139(5) treats as arrest` `the official was exercising a border, search or information power` `the official could arrest me` `the official believed there was enough evidence I had committed an offence` `I was cautioned in a language I speak reasonably fluently` `a law required me to answer`)
    THEN `s 138 does not exclude it: it was not obtained improperly or illegally, or in consequence of an impropriety or illegality`
    ELSE IF `the court finds admitting it is more desirable`
         THEN `no: it was obtained improperly or illegally, but the court finds the desirability of admitting it outweighs the undesirability of admitting evidence obtained that way, so it may be admitted (s 138(1), (3))`
         ELSE `yes: it was obtained improperly or illegally, and it is not to be admitted, because the court does not find that the desirability of admitting it outweighs the undesirability of admitting evidence obtained that way (s 138(1))`

§§ `ss 94, 97, 98, 100, 101 -- if the prosecution wants to use my past conduct (tendency or coincidence)`

-- s 94: Part 3.6 does not apply "(1) to evidence that relates only to the credibility of a witness", "(2) so far as a
-- proceeding relates to bail or sentencing", or (3) where the character, conduct or tendency "is a fact in issue".
-- s 97(1), the tendency rule: evidence of "character, reputation or conduct ..., or a tendency" is "not admissible to prove
-- that a person has or had a tendency ... to act in a particular way, or to have a particular state of mind unless: (a) the
-- party ... gave reasonable notice in writing to each other party ...; and (b) the court thinks that the evidence will ...
-- have significant probative value." (2): no notice is needed if adduced under a s 100 direction or "to explain or contradict
-- tendency evidence adduced by another party". s 98: the same for coincidence evidence ("it is improbable that the events
-- occurred coincidentally"). s 101(2): "Tendency evidence about a defendant, or coincidence evidence about a defendant, that is
-- adduced by the prosecution cannot be used against the defendant unless the probative value of the evidence substantially
-- outweighs any prejudicial effect it may have on the defendant"; (3), (4): not where the prosecution adduces it "to explain or
-- contradict" my own tendency or coincidence evidence.
DECLARE `Can my past conduct be used against me` IS ONE OF
    `Part 3.6 does not apply: the evidence goes only to a witness's credibility, or the hearing is about bail or sentence, or my conduct or tendency is itself a fact in issue (s 94)`
    `no: the prosecution did not give me reasonable written notice and the court has not dispensed with it, so the rule excludes it (ss 97(1)(a), 98(1)(a), 100)`
    `no: the court does not think it will have significant probative value, so the rule excludes it (ss 97(1)(b), 98(1)(b))`
    `no: its probative value does not substantially outweigh its prejudicial effect on me, so it cannot be used against me (s 101(2))`
    `yes: it passes the tendency or coincidence rule and may be used against me (ss 97, 98, 101)`

GIVEN `it goes only to credibility, bail or sentence, or is itself in issue` IS A BOOLEAN
      `it answers tendency or coincidence evidence I adduced` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the prosecution gave me written notice or the court excused it` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the court thinks it has significant probative value` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the court finds its probative value substantially outweighs the prejudice to me` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Can my past conduct be used against me`
`the tendency or coincidence rule` MEANS
    IF `it goes only to credibility, bail or sentence, or is itself in issue`
    THEN `Part 3.6 does not apply: the evidence goes only to a witness's credibility, or the hearing is about bail or sentence, or my conduct or tendency is itself a fact in issue (s 94)`
    ELSE IF (NOT `the prosecution gave me written notice or the court excused it`) AND (NOT `it answers tendency or coincidence evidence I adduced`)
         THEN `no: the prosecution did not give me reasonable written notice and the court has not dispensed with it, so the rule excludes it (ss 97(1)(a), 98(1)(a), 100)`
         ELSE IF (NOT `the court thinks it has significant probative value`)
              THEN `no: the court does not think it will have significant probative value, so the rule excludes it (ss 97(1)(b), 98(1)(b))`
              ELSE IF (NOT `it answers tendency or coincidence evidence I adduced`) AND (NOT `the court finds its probative value substantially outweighs the prejudice to me`)
                   THEN `no: its probative value does not substantially outweigh its prejudicial effect on me, so it cannot be used against me (s 101(2))`
                   ELSE `yes: it passes the tendency or coincidence rule and may be used against me (ss 97, 98, 101)`

@export Can the prosecution use my past conduct or character to show I have a tendency to act that way (tendency evidence)?
GIVEN `it goes only to credibility, bail or sentence, or is itself in issue` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the evidence goes only to the credibility of a witness, or the hearing is about bail or sentence, or your character, conduct or tendency is itself a fact the case is about
      `it answers tendency evidence I adduced` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the prosecution is adducing it to explain or contradict tendency evidence that you adduced
      `the prosecution gave me written notice or the court excused it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the prosecution gave you reasonable written notice that it would adduce the evidence, or the court directed under s 100 that notice is not needed
      `the court thinks it has significant probative value` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court thinks the evidence will, by itself or with other prosecution evidence, have significant probative value
      `the court finds its probative value substantially outweighs the prejudice to me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court finds that the probative value of the evidence substantially outweighs any prejudicial effect it may have on you
GIVETH A `Can my past conduct be used against me`
`Can my past conduct be used as tendency evidence` MEANS
    `the tendency or coincidence rule` `it goes only to credibility, bail or sentence, or is itself in issue` `it answers tendency evidence I adduced` `the prosecution gave me written notice or the court excused it` `the court thinks it has significant probative value` `the court finds its probative value substantially outweighs the prejudice to me`

@export Can the prosecution rely on similar events to argue it is improbable they happened by coincidence (coincidence evidence)?
GIVEN `it goes only to credibility, bail or sentence, or is itself in issue` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the evidence goes only to the credibility of a witness, or the hearing is about bail or sentence, or your conduct is itself a fact the case is about
      `it answers coincidence evidence I adduced` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the prosecution is adducing it to explain or contradict coincidence evidence that you adduced
      `the prosecution gave me written notice or the court excused it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the prosecution gave you reasonable written notice that it would adduce the evidence, or the court directed under s 100 that notice is not needed
      `the court thinks it has significant probative value` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court thinks the evidence will, by itself or with other prosecution evidence, have significant probative value
      `the court finds its probative value substantially outweighs the prejudice to me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court finds that the probative value of the evidence substantially outweighs any prejudicial effect it may have on you
GIVETH A `Can my past conduct be used against me`
`Can similar events be used as coincidence evidence` MEANS
    `the tendency or coincidence rule` `it goes only to credibility, bail or sentence, or is itself in issue` `it answers coincidence evidence I adduced` `the prosecution gave me written notice or the court excused it` `the court thinks it has significant probative value` `the court finds its probative value substantially outweighs the prejudice to me`

§§ `ss 110, 112 -- if I call evidence that I am of good character`

-- s 110(1): the hearsay, opinion, tendency and credibility rules "do not apply to evidence adduced by a defendant to prove ...
-- that the defendant is, either generally or in a particular respect, a person of good character." (2): once evidence that I
-- am "generally" of good character is admitted, those rules do not stop evidence that I am "not generally" of good character;
-- (3): if it was "in a particular respect", only evidence that I am not of good character "in that respect". s 112: "A
-- defendant must not be cross-examined about matters arising out of evidence of a kind referred to in this Part unless the
-- court gives leave."
DECLARE `What can the prosecution do if I call good character evidence` IS ONE OF
    `nothing under s 110: I have not had evidence of my good character admitted, so the rules stop the prosecution leading my bad character to show a tendency (s 97), subject to the tendency rule`
    `it may answer with evidence that I am not generally of good character (s 110(2)); I may be cross-examined about it only with the court's leave (s 112)`
    `it may answer only with evidence that I am not of good character in that particular respect (s 110(3)); I may be cross-examined about it only with the court's leave (s 112)`

@export If I call evidence that I am of good character, can the prosecution answer with my bad character?
GIVEN `evidence of my good character has been admitted` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether evidence you adduced to show that you are a person of good character has been admitted
      `it was about my character generally` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether that evidence was about your good character generally (TRUE) or only in a particular respect, such as honesty (FALSE)
GIVETH A `What can the prosecution do if I call good character evidence`
`What can the prosecution do if I call good character evidence question` MEANS
    IF (NOT `evidence of my good character has been admitted`)
    THEN `nothing under s 110: I have not had evidence of my good character admitted, so the rules stop the prosecution leading my bad character to show a tendency (s 97), subject to the tendency rule`
    ELSE IF `it was about my character generally`
         THEN `it may answer with evidence that I am not generally of good character (s 110(2)); I may be cross-examined about it only with the court's leave (s 112)`
         ELSE `it may answer only with evidence that I am not of good character in that particular respect (s 110(3)); I may be cross-examined about it only with the court's leave (s 112)`

§§ `ss 135, 137, 141 -- unfair prejudice, and what the prosecution must prove`

-- s 137: "In a criminal proceeding, the court must refuse to admit evidence adduced by the prosecutor if its probative value is
-- outweighed by the danger of unfair prejudice to the defendant." s 135: the court "may refuse to admit evidence if its
-- probative value is substantially outweighed by the danger that the evidence might: (a) be unfairly prejudicial to a party; or
-- (b) be misleading or confusing; or (c) cause or result in undue waste of time." The weighing is the court's.
DECLARE `Must the court exclude prejudicial prosecution evidence` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the court must refuse it, because its probative value is outweighed by the danger of unfair prejudice to me (s 137)`
    `it may: the court has refused it in its discretion, because its probative value is substantially outweighed by the danger of unfair prejudice, of misleading or confusing, or of undue waste of time (s 135)`
    `no: neither s 137 nor s 135 excludes it`

@export Must the court keep out prosecution evidence that is unfairly prejudicial to me?
GIVEN `the court finds its probative value is outweighed by unfair prejudice to me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court finds that the probative value of the prosecution's evidence is outweighed by the danger of unfair prejudice to you
      `the court refuses it in its discretion under s 135` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court finds its probative value substantially outweighed by the danger that it may be unfairly prejudicial, misleading or confusing, or waste time, and refuses it
GIVETH A `Must the court exclude prejudicial prosecution evidence`
`Must the court exclude prejudicial prosecution evidence question` MEANS
    IF `the court finds its probative value is outweighed by unfair prejudice to me`
    THEN `yes: the court must refuse it, because its probative value is outweighed by the danger of unfair prejudice to me (s 137)`
    ELSE IF `the court refuses it in its discretion under s 135`
         THEN `it may: the court has refused it in its discretion, because its probative value is substantially outweighed by the danger of unfair prejudice, of misleading or confusing, or of undue waste of time (s 135)`
         ELSE `no: neither s 137 nor s 135 excludes it`

-- s 141(1): "In a criminal proceeding, the court is not to find the case of the prosecution proved unless it is satisfied that
-- it has been proved beyond reasonable doubt." (2): "the court is to find the case of a defendant proved if it is satisfied
-- that it has been proved on the balance of probabilities." Dictionary: "case of a party means the facts in issue in respect of
-- which the party bears the legal burden of proof." Who bears which burden is for the law creating the offence (for a
-- Commonwealth offence, Criminal Code Part 2.6), not this Act.
DECLARE `To what standard must it be proved` IS ONE OF
    `beyond reasonable doubt: it is part of the prosecution's case (s 141(1))`
    `on the balance of probabilities: it is a fact on which I bear the legal burden of proof (s 141(2))`

@export How sure must the court be before it finds a fact proved against me, or in my favour?
GIVEN `I bear the legal burden of proving this fact` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the law creating the offence puts the legal burden of proving this fact on you (for a Commonwealth offence, only where the law says so expressly, Criminal Code s 13.4); FALSE if it is a fact the prosecution must prove
GIVETH A `To what standard must it be proved`
`To what standard must it be proved question` MEANS
    IF `I bear the legal burden of proving this fact`
    THEN `on the balance of probabilities: it is a fact on which I bear the legal burden of proof (s 141(2))`
    ELSE `beyond reasonable doubt: it is part of the prosecution's case (s 141(1))`
